      "body": "## Purpose\r\n\r\nFixes #57030.\r\n\r\nTwo Triton kernels compute row offsets with `int32` arithmetic. With large\r\nrequest buffers, an offset can exceed `2**31 - 1` and wrap to a negative value.\r\nThe staged-write kernel can then write outside the buffer, and the penalty\r\nkernel can read outside it.\r\n\r\n## Change\r\n\r\n- `_apply_write_kernel` casts `row_idx` to `tl.int64` before it multiplies by\r\n  the row stride.\r\n- `_bincount_kernel` casts the request index to `tl.int64` before the stride\r\n  calculations.\r\n\r\nThe index buffers stay `int32`. Only the address calculations change.\r\n\r\n## Test plan\r\n\r\nBoth new tests use a `(65536, 32769)` `int32` buffer. The last row starts at\r\nelement `2147516415`, just past the signed 32-bit limit.\r\n\r\nI tested commit `a46940b5e4` on one NVIDIA L4. For the baseline check, I kept\r\nthe new tests and restored `buffer_utils.py` and `sample/penalties.py` from\r\n`d5f0a6e82`. Each test ran in a separate process, and both failed with a CUDA\r\nillegal memory access.\r\n\r\nAfter I restored the PR code, the full UVA test file and the new bincount\r\ntest passed: **21 passed, no skips, in 10.59 seconds**.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\npython -m pytest -v -s -rs \\\r\n  tests/kernels/core/test_uva.py \\\r\n  tests/v1/worker/test_gpu_bincount_i64.py\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe test environment was Ubuntu 22.04, Python 3.12.14, PyTorch 2.13.0+cu130,\r\nand Triton 3.7.1. I used an editable source install with the precompiled CUDA\r\nextensions from `d5f0a6e82`, plus `cuda-compat-13-0`.\r\nNo import shim was needed. \r\n",
